<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali ibn Mahdi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1159</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> was a preacher from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a>, the coastal plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, who denounced the luxury of the Najahid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> and gathered a following among the tribes from the 1130s. After years of raids he captured Zabid in 1159, ending the Najahid dynasty, and died the same year. His sons ruled as the Mahdid dynasty until the Ayyubid conquest of 1174.</p></div>
